hi there steve kaufman here  today i want to talk about how  language learning is  very much a subjective  activity and all of  you know of what that implies  um  and the reason this thought came to me  is  uh you know obviously we have this  coveted epidemic now and um  you know what affects your  ability to contract the disease and how  severely you will be affected by  the disease depends on a number of  factors many of which we don't yet know  and and as  the scientists learn more about the  covet they'll have a better idea of  different people's vulnerability and and  what kinds of situations are most  dangerous  they know a lot already some people who  have certain medical conditions or of a  certain age are more likely to be  you know adversely affected by covid and  yet there are surprises there are young  apparently very healthy people who are  very severely affected by covet  now language learning is different in  the case of covid there are certain  we don't know what all the factors are  but there are some objective factors  that we don't control  that will affect how we are you know uh  how badly we're impacted by cobit but  language learning is not like that you  know our ability to learn languages it  does vary from person to person  but  we also influence those things so it's  not a purely sort of objective factor i  am obese or i am of a certain age and  therefore i'm statistically more likely  to  contract a severe form of covet  in terms of language learning  i can  control  the sort of emotional  uh attitudinal part of language learning  is very very important  and unfortunately many of the studies  that are done  on what works in language learning and  i've been doing a bit of research they  have a term gll good language learners  and it all deals with  either techniques in the classroom that  the teacher can use  or techniques that learners supposedly  use like they're more inclined to guess  is  suggested as one  characteristic of good language learners  um i don't really guess i think i'm a  fairly good language learner  i try not to guess  um  ultimately  it's a matter of you know how motivated  are we they do these studies of  uh how long does it take to learn a  language how long does it take  uh  you know to learn different languages  and it's all based on a classroom  situation i remember some years ago  seeing a study done by uh in the united  states showing how  classroom hours affect  you know how quickly immigrants learn  english but of course there's so many  factors that have nothing to do with the  classroom uh is the particular immigrant  you know working in an environment where  there are other english speakers  does that person watch english-speaking  television programs uh  does that person react positively to  english-speaking television programs or  do they kind of not really like the  culture nothing wrong with not liking  any particular culture but obviously  those kinds of subjective things are  going to influence how easily they learn  the language  so  and i've had a number of discussions  recently interviews with people we talk  about what affects language learning and  it's not possible to like everything in  a in a new culture  but it should be possible to find some  things that we like  or people that we like or voices that we  like or subject matter that we like so  that we create as positive a subjective  environment as possible you can't just  tell people go and find friends in the  language if those people are not  positively attracted to the language  so  you know it once we recognize the  subjective nature of  language learning not only in terms of  initiative by the language learner but  also in terms of  emotionally how the learner reacts to  the language what the interests of the  learner are  it can also be a matter of confidence  obviously a person who has learned more  languages is more confident so that's a  subjective factor  i i don't think it's so much a matter of  the language learning techniques that  good language learners use i think it's  rather that the good language learners  allow themselves to get carried away  with a language find things interesting  in the language  and then in their desire to learn more  about the language and the culture they  will naturally find  certain techniques  there that that work they're more  willing to accept uncertainty because  they're so interested in what they're  reading or listening to or interested in  certain people so a lot of the sort of  techniques you know accepting  uncertainty  guessing or not guessing whatever  accepting the fact that you're going to  forget  and relearn  accepting the fact that you're going to  be embarrassed because you can't  remember things or you have to ask  someone to repeat things and all of  these sort of inhibiting factors  if we subjectively have an attitude that  says i'm interested i like it i like the  sound  then we sort of  are prepared to  fight our way through some of these  negative things so if the person  subjectively  doesn't really like being with speakers  of that language which is understandable  so a lot of these subjective things are  going to inhibit their success  so  whenever i see studies about what works  in language learning typically based on  the classroom experience  where i find that the classroom is  almost irrelevant to language learning  whether a learner is in a classroom or  not is the other  the things that the learner does outside  the classroom which are largely dictated  by the learner's attitude towards the  language or towards the process of  learning in other words subjective  factors  and  these subjective factors i think should  be studied in greater detail to see how  we can create more  subjective positive  attitudes towards a particular language  or towards the process of language  learning that rather than any techniques  of language learning or language  teaching i think have a much greater  influence so there you have it i look  forward to your comments  [Music]  you
 

